  "summary_proposal": {
    "id": "use-case-summary-somatic-small-variant-oncogenicity",
    "field": "summary",
    "status": "needs_review",
    "value": "Two new comparisons join the existing OncoVI evidence; values cannot be pooled across them. In Chen et al. 2020, a group that developed none of the 33 predictors scored somatic missense mutations labelled by OncoKB or by cell viability assays, with somatic or assay-neutral negatives. Ranking by each tool's median score, the best accuracy was 0.69 on OncoKB labels (PROVEAN, VEST4) and 0.70 on the assay labels (CTAT-cancer, PrimateAI; CHASM 0.69); FATHMM-disease was near chance (0.50 on OncoKB). Using the tools' own default calls, accuracy on OncoKB was at most 0.70 (DEOGEN2, sensitivity 0.87, specificity 0.51), and several tools called most neutral mutations damaging, for example M-CAP with specificity 0.11 and CanDrA 0.17. In a 2026 preprint by one author (Lee), 49 scores were compared on Cancer Gene Census missense variants with germline-benign negatives, which this use case does not accept as somatic controls. Most tools scored oncogene variants lower than tumour-suppressor variants, for example AlphaMissense AUROC 0.88 versus 0.90 and CADD 0.82 versus 0.89, while popEVE went the other way (0.90 versus 0.84). The highest all-gene AUROC was 0.88 (MutScore). Values are rounded to two decimals. None of these benchmarks measures calibrated probabilities, the effect of a variant on drug response, or reviewer agreement.",
